host definitions

\ Buffered compilation =======================================================
\ What's actually getting collected during compilation of a nonprimitive is a
\ doubly-linked list of abstract operations. These are platform-specific,
\ arbitrary structures that hold whatever details are necessary for final
\ draining into machine code.

\ They're a doubly-linked list because it makes splicing easy: nodes can be
\ optimized away, a call replaced with its inlined definition, etc. The nodes
\ are compiler ephemera and don't survive to the target.

\ The `mirror>compile` xts append these nodes to the current definition's list,
\ rather than emitting machine code directly. At the end of a definition, the
\ compiler will drain the entries into machine code. The granularity of
\ compilation is still a single definition, since we need real addresses and so
\ on between definitions.

\ Defining words like `CONSTANT` need logic in their `HOST ACTS:` that will
\ adjust the `mirror>compile` of the target words to be a platform-specific
\ hook for the use of the compiler. That can be a simple `DEFER`ed word in the
\ mirror vocab, defaulting to a vanilla `mirror>compile` call like it does now.

\ So I think that's the interface here; the things that a `core` or `kernel`
\ for each platform must define in order to integrate with the metacompiler:
\ - Primitives are defined with a `mirror>compile` that does the right thing.
\ - Override the default `mirror>compile` for all `CREATE`d words.
\ - Override a hook, called from `;` and friends, that ends a definition.
\   - There may already be something like this in the model, `EXIT,` or so?

\ To support e.g. inlining, we need to be able to map between target xts and
\ the buffered words. The easiest way to do that is to have a pointer to the
\ latest word, and it has a dictionary-like chain of pointers to all the
\ previous words.

\ Reusable buffering machinery first:
VARIABLE compiling       \ The word being compiled. 0 when not compiling a word.
VARIABLE last-compiled   \ Points to the bufword most recently created.


begin-structure [bufword]
  \ The previous compiled word. Forms a complete chain with `last-compiled`.
  FIELD: bufword>prev
  FIELD: bufword>txt    \ Target xt for this word.
  FIELD: bufword>head   \ Pointers to both ends of the linked list of nodes.
  FIELD: bufword>tail
end-structure

\ Begins compilation of a new word. Throws if there is already a word open,
\ since that indicates a user bug.
: begin-comp ( -- )
  compiling @ ABORT" Cannot start a new compilation with another open"
  [bufword] allocate throw
  \ Link it to the chain.
  last-compiled @   over bufword>prev !
  \ Start it at the current `code-space`.
  code-space @   over bufword>txt !
  \ And it is both the most recent compilation and the current one.
  dup last-compiled !
  compiling ! ;


\ Prefix of all buffered nodes, with the linked list chain, a type flag, and
\ the xt to call to assemble the node.
begin-structure [bufnode]
  FIELD: bufnode>next
  FIELD: bufnode>prev
  FIELD: bufnode>type
  FIELD: bufnode>assemble  \ ( bufnode -- ) that assembles the necessary code
end-structure

\ Compiles the entire sequence of nodes until the node>next is 0.
: drain-node ( bufnode -- )
  dup bufnode>assemble @ execute ;

CREATE node-printers 16 cells allot

: PRINTER  ( type -- 'xt ) cells node-printers + ;
: PRINTER! ( xt type -- )  printer ! ;

: .node ( bufnode -- )
  ." Node " dup hex.
  ." @ " code-space @ hex.
  ." of type " dup bufnode>type @   dup .
  printer @ execute    cr ;


: drain-nodes ( bufnode -- )
  ." =========================================" cr
  ." Draining node " dup hex. ." at: " code-space @ hex. cr
  BEGIN dup WHILE
    dup .node
    dup drain-node
    bufnode>next @
  REPEAT drop ;

\ Closes compilation of the current word. Does not add any return or exit, so
\ that must already have been included.
: done-comp ( -- )
  compiling @ 0= ABORT" Cannot close compilation when none is open"
  \ Drain all the nodes into the machine code.
  compiling @ bufword>head @ drain-nodes
  compiling OFF ;

\ Links two nodes, so that left comes before right. DOES NOT update the "outer"
\ pointers, only the "inner" ones that refer to each other.
\ Safely handles either of the would-be pointers being 0.
: xlink ( left right -- )
  2dup   dup IF bufnode>prev ! ELSE 2drop THEN
  swap   dup IF bufnode>next ! ELSE 2drop THEN ;

\ Linked list insert-before.
: insert-before { noob host -- }
  host bufnode>prev @ noob xlink    \ host's old `prev` now followed by noob,
  noob                host xlink ;  \ and noob followed by host.

: insert-after { noob host -- }
  noob    host bufnode>next @ xlink   \ host's old `next` now preceded by noob,
  host    noob                xlink ; \ and host now followed by noob.


: !compiling ( -- ) compiling @ 0= ABORT" Cannot comp+ outside a definition" ;

\ Appends the given node to the current definition.
: comp+ ( bufnode -- )
  !compiling
  compiling @ bufword>tail @ ?dup IF ( bufnode tail )
    over compiling @ bufword>tail ! ( bufnode tail )
    insert-after ( )
  ELSE ( bufnode )
    dup compiling @ bufword>head 2! \ Both the head and tail.
  THEN ( ) ;


\ Creates and appends a new bufnode with the given type and assemble-xt.
: new-bufnode ( type assemble-xt size -- bufnode )
  allocate throw ( type assemble-xt bufnode )
  dup comp+
  tuck bufnode>assemble ! ( type bufnode )
  tuck bufnode>type ! ;


\ Caller nodes ===============================================================
begin-structure [caller]
  [bufnode] +
  FIELD: caller>t-xt
  FIELD: caller>slot   \ Address where the real target address went.
                       \ Used for resolving forward references.
end-structure

2 CONSTANT buftype-caller

:noname ( caller -- )
  ." CALLER " caller>t-xt @ hex.
; buftype-caller PRINTER!

\ bufnode>assemble word for true, JSR-based calls, as opposed to inlined ones.
\ This corresponds to a vanilla `tcompile,` on a non-buffered platform.
DEFER assemble-caller ( bufnode -- )

: call, ( t-xt -- caller-bufnode )
  buftype-caller ['] assemble-caller [caller] new-bufnode
  tuck caller>t-xt ! ;

\ Create and append a node for a call to a given t-xt.
: tcompile, ( t-xt -- )
  call, drop ;

\ Forward references =========================================================
\ Compile a call to a forward ref, returning an opaque cell-sized token.
DEFER forward-ref ( -- token )
DEFER resolve-ref ( token real-t-xt -- )


\ Literal nodes ==============================================================
begin-structure [literal]
  [bufnode] +
  FIELD: literal>value
  FIELD: literal>instr
  FIELD: literal>pool-slot
end-structure

3 CONSTANT buftype-literal

:noname ( literal -- )
  ." LITERAL " literal>value @ dup . hex.
; buftype-literal PRINTER!

\ bufnode>assemble word for pushing a literal onto the stack.
DEFER assemble-literal ( literal-node -- )

\ Appends a [literal] bufword for the given literal.
: tliteral ( x --    X: -- x )
  [literal] allocate throw
  dup comp+
  buftype-literal    over bufnode>type !
  ['] assemble-literal over bufnode>assemble !
  literal>value ! ;

\ Start, end, dodoes nodes ===================================================
\ Nodes for the start and end of a nonprimitive word. Also for the handling of
\ CREATEd words with a DOES> block.
4 CONSTANT buftype-starter
5 CONSTANT buftype-return
6 CONSTANT buftype-dodoes

:noname ( buftype -- ) drop ." STARTER" ; buftype-starter PRINTER!
:noname ( buftype -- ) drop ." RETURN"  ; buftype-return  PRINTER!
:noname ( buftype -- ) drop ." DODOES"  ; buftype-dodoes  PRINTER!

DEFER assemble-starter ( starter-bufnode -- )
DEFER assemble-return  ( return-bufnode  -- )
DEFER assemble-dodoes  ( dodoes-bufnode  -- )

: +starter ( -- )
  buftype-starter ['] assemble-starter [bufnode] new-bufnode drop ;
: +return  ( -- )
  buftype-return  ['] assemble-return  [bufnode] new-bufnode drop ;
: +dodoes  ( -- )
  buftype-dodoes  ['] assemble-dodoes  [bufnode] new-bufnode drop ;

\ Locations, values and logical stacks =======================================
\ Values are either opaque or literals. Opaque values are unique in the
\ compiler's memory. Values can exist in one or more *locations*, with one of
\ those locations considered "canonical".

\ Consider `DUP` - logically it creates a copy of a value, but it does not need
\ to be directly realized in the code. Any operation might destroy a register,
\ or might preserve it, and that's platform-specific. So a `DUP` might be
\ realized by popping the stack into a register, using it non-destructively
\ twice, and then not mentioning it again.
\ Each *usage* of a value in an operation can flip through the various aliases
\ (= locations) where the value can be found and can use whichever of them is
\ most convenient.
\ TODO: Is a "ref count" useful here? We need to know if a value can be
\ destroyed or if something still needs it.

\ Literals are fully abstract, and should support things like constant folding.
\ They don't get realized until they are used, since it's a last-minute call
\ whether it goes in a literal field, a constant pool, etc.

\ Locations are one of:
\ - The real data stack
\   - Addressed using an offset relative to the SP at the start of this word.
\ - The real return stack
\   - Likewise, offset from RSP at the start.
\ - A real register (e.g. `lr` holds the return address)
\ - An abstract register, yet to be allocated.

\ So on entry to a non-primitive, the logical return stack holds the return
\ address, whose proper location is in the real `lr` register.

REQUIRE objects.fs \ The Gforth library


\ Locations ==================================================================
\ These track the various places a value can live, and point to their value.
object class
  cell% field loc-contents \ Pointer to the Value object.
  selector register? ( loc -- ? )
end-class location

?stack-clear

location class
  double% field reg-operand
  :noname ( loc -- ? ) true ; overrides register?

  :noname ( operand loc -- ) reg-operand 2! ; overrides construct
end-class register

?stack-clear
\ : new-lr ( -- loc ) lr register heap-new ;

\ The offset is in cells, where 0 is TOS at the start of a non-primitive, and
\ 1 represents NOS. Negative values are those added to the stack later. The
\ compiler also keeps track of the stack pointer as the same kind of offset.
\ Therefore the true location in memory is actually (offset - sp) cells from
\ the hardware SP.
location class
  cell% field offset
end-class abs-stacked

abs-stacked class   end-class stacked
abs-stacked class   end-class return-stacked

?stack-clear

\ Values =====================================================================
\ Abstract value descriptors. These have a set of 0 or more locations, of which
\ one is considered canonical. The exception is that a literal might not have
\ a location, if nothing ever needs it to have one. Literals are sometimes not
\ reified, instead getting used directly as immediate operands. If a literal has
\ to go in a literal pool, that becomes its canonical location.
object class
  cell% field canonical   \ Canonincal location
  cell% field aliases     \ A linked list of aliased locations
end-class value-descriptor

value-descriptor class
  cell% field the-value
end-class litvalue

value-descriptor class
end-class opaque-value



\ Compiler state =============================================================
\ Bookkeeping state for the compiler when compiling a non-primitive.

8 CONSTANT /comp-stacking
object class
  cell% inst-var hw-offset
  cell% inst-var stack
  m: ( stack -- )
    \ Allocate /comp-stacking cells in both directions.
    /comp-stacking 2* cells allocate throw
    \ Then point into the middle
    /comp-stacking cells +    ( 'tos )
    stack !
    0 hw-offset !
  ;m overrides construct

  \ Gets the *pointer* to the value descriptor at the given depth a value at the given depth on this stack. The depth is relative to the
  \ current notional stack pointer, not the hardware offset.
  :m at ( depth stack -- 'value-descriptor )
    cells stack @ + ;m

  :m pickx ( depth stack -- value-descriptor )
    this at @ ;m

  :m popx ( stack -- value-descriptor )
    0 this pickx   cell stack +! ;m

  :m pushx ( value-descriptor stack -- )
    -1 cells stack +!   0 this at ! ;m
end-class comp-stack

?stack-clear
object class
  inst-value data-stack
  inst-value return-stack
  inst-value literal-pool    \ Linked list of literals in the pool.

  :noname ( cs -- )
    comp-stack heap-new [to-inst] data-stack
    comp-stack heap-new [to-inst] return-stack
    0 [to-inst] literal-pool
  ; overrides construct
end-class comp-state

?stack-clear
